########################################################################
Start of comments

This planetary constants kernel updates parameters for the Pluto system
relative to what are found in the current NAIF-provided PCK file
"pck00011.tpc". To use this kernel, furnsh it after you have furnshed
either pck00010 or pck00011.

This file contains updated sizes, orientation, and spin states for Pluto
and its moons as derived from New Horizons imaging. It also defines the
rotation states for Pluto, Charon, Nix, and Hydra at the time of the flyby
in July 2015 (although note that these bodies may not be undergoing simple,
fixed rotation).

The updates to the orientation and spin states for Pluto and Charon correct
a 1.45 degree error in the prime meridians of these two bodies.

The corrections to the Pluto and Charon rotation states allow compatibility
with the latest system ephemeris, PLU055. That ephemeris was described by
Jacobson et al. 2015 (7); it updates the pre-flyby analysis by Brozovic
et al. 2015 (8).

Pluto and Charon are tidally locked to one another. The IAU has defined the
prime meridian of Pluto as the mean sub-Charon meridian, and the prime
meridian of Charon as the mean sub-Pluto meridian. See
    https://astrogeology.usgs.gov/search/map/Docs/WGCCRE/WGCCRE2009reprint
for details.

In the pre-existing SPICE PCKs, the values defining the rotation states of
Pluto and Charon are fixed constants, 56.3625225 degrees per day, and have
not been updated since at least 1994. The correct value, based on PLU055,
is 56.3625276 degrees per day. This discrepancy, plus a zero-point error,
results in a sub-Charon longitude on Pluto, and a sub-Pluto longitude on
Charon, of 1.45 degrees as of the New Horizons flyby in July 2015. Without
a correction, that error will increase at 0.2 degrees per century going
forward.

This PCK defines rotation states that are consisent with the orginal IAU
intent to orient the mean prime meridian of each body toward the other.
Because the PLU055 orbital elements have not been published, the values
provided here were derived by fitting the rotation parameters so as to
minimize the variations in the sub-Pluto position on Charon and vice-versa.
During 1950-2050, these values maintain the alignment of the meridians
to < 0.02 degrees and alignment of the equators to < 0.03 degrees.

The New Horizons team will be working with the IAU to adopt these more
accurate rotation models for Pluto and Charon. Once they are adopted, they
will appear in the PCK file maintained by the NAIF team. However, as of
this writing, the contents of pck00011.tpc have already been finalized and
will not include this correction.

########################################################################

